WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:08.680
All right, everyone, now we're going to talk about how to control the flow in your stored procedure.

00:08.680 --> 00:11.880
And we're going to learn how to do that using the if else statement.

00:11.920 --> 00:13.560
So now let's have the following scenario.

00:13.760 --> 00:17.280
Now if you check our query over here we are doing the average of score.

00:17.480 --> 00:21.600
And if you check the data you can see that in the scores we have nulls.

00:21.640 --> 00:24.000
And nulls are really bad for aggregations.

00:24.000 --> 00:28.480
So we usually have to clean up our data before doing any aggregations.

00:28.480 --> 00:33.240
And in this scenario we can understand null as a zero and how we're going to clean up and handle the

00:33.240 --> 00:33.760
data.

00:33.920 --> 00:39.520
We're going to go and make an update on our table where we say if there is a NULL, then make it as

00:39.520 --> 00:40.160
a zero.

00:40.160 --> 00:44.040
And we will do this as a pre step inside our stored procedure.

00:44.040 --> 00:46.760
So that means first we have to clean up the data.

00:46.760 --> 00:49.560
And then afterwards we're going to generate the reports.

00:49.560 --> 00:52.680
And this is what we usually do inside SQL projects.

00:52.680 --> 00:54.480
So the logic can be very simple.

00:54.480 --> 00:55.520
We have to check first.

00:55.560 --> 00:57.480
Do we have nulls inside the score.

00:57.520 --> 01:01.840
If the answer is yes, then we have to go and update the null values to zero.

01:02.080 --> 01:06.080
But if the answer is no, we don't have any values, then we can skip everything.

01:06.080 --> 01:11.340
So now we're going to go and build this logic inside our stored procedure in order to clean up and prepare

01:11.340 --> 01:11.900
the data.

01:11.940 --> 01:12.740
So let's go.

01:12.780 --> 01:13.180
Okay.

01:13.220 --> 01:18.260
So now this part we're going to call it generating reports.

01:19.260 --> 01:25.060
And we're going to have another part called prepare and clean up data.

01:25.100 --> 01:28.660
So now let's prepare first the structure of the if else statements.

01:28.660 --> 01:30.220
So the syntax is going to look like this.

01:30.220 --> 01:32.900
So if and then begin and end.

01:33.540 --> 01:37.460
So this is the block of the if and we're going to do the same thing for the else.

01:37.460 --> 01:40.580
So we have else and we have begin and end.

01:40.620 --> 01:41.940
Let me just separate them.

01:41.940 --> 01:44.780
So now how this works we have to create a condition.

01:45.060 --> 01:48.820
If the condition is met then the if statement can be executed.

01:48.820 --> 01:54.300
But if the condition is not fulfilled and we have false, then the else statement is going to be executed.

01:54.300 --> 01:55.340
So what is the condition?

01:55.340 --> 01:58.700
We have to check whether there is null inside the scores.

01:58.740 --> 02:00.420
So let's write a very simple query.

02:00.460 --> 02:09.300
It's going to say select one from sales customers where score is null.

02:09.780 --> 02:12.000
And always we have to check the country 93.

02:12.560 --> 02:14.360
Equal to let's say USA.

02:14.520 --> 02:16.920
So let's go and execute this one over here.

02:17.120 --> 02:19.200
So now we are getting the output results.

02:19.200 --> 02:23.040
If you are getting a results that means somewhere there are nulls.

02:23.040 --> 02:29.200
But if you go for example and say here Germany and execute the semi query in the output, you see that

02:29.200 --> 02:30.320
we don't have any results.

02:30.320 --> 02:35.240
That means for the German customers, we don't have any nulls in their scores.

02:35.400 --> 02:38.200
So if this query returns something, we have nulls.

02:38.200 --> 02:41.240
If it didn't return anything then there is no nulls.

02:41.280 --> 02:44.360
And we can use exactly this query as a condition.

02:44.560 --> 02:50.000
So we're going to take our check and say if exists and then two parentheses.

02:50.000 --> 02:51.480
And then we put our query.

02:51.480 --> 02:57.720
So what we are saying if exists if this query return anything then go and execute the next block.

02:57.720 --> 03:01.240
And if it is not exist that means it is not returning anything.

03:01.240 --> 03:03.480
Then go and execute the second block.

03:03.480 --> 03:04.400
So it's a logic, right?

03:04.400 --> 03:05.240
It's very simple.

03:05.280 --> 03:08.960
Now of course, instead of having a static value over here we can use our parameter.

03:08.960 --> 03:10.800
So add country.

03:10.840 --> 03:14.520
And now we have to tell SQL what to do if it exists.

03:14.520 --> 03:17.240
So in between we can have like an update statement.

03:17.240 --> 03:23.730
So updates sales customers and we're going to set the score equal to zero.

03:23.770 --> 03:26.890
But very important we have to go and use where condition.

03:26.890 --> 03:29.250
Otherwise it's going to go and update everything.

03:29.450 --> 03:36.450
The score is null and the country equal to our parameter country.

03:37.250 --> 03:40.850
So with that we are updating exactly the nulls for specific country.

03:40.890 --> 03:43.090
And let's have a semicolon at the end.

03:43.250 --> 03:47.770
And at the start maybe I'm going to say just to have a nice message in the output print.

03:47.770 --> 03:55.690
And we can have a message updating null scores to zero and as well a semicolon at the end.

03:55.690 --> 04:01.090
So if there is any nulls then execute the whole thing, print the message and update the table.

04:01.130 --> 04:05.330
So now the next step is that we're going to go and tell SQL what can happen if the condition is not

04:05.330 --> 04:05.970
fulfilled.

04:06.170 --> 04:08.050
That means we don't have any nulls.

04:08.050 --> 04:12.250
Well we don't have to update the table at all because we don't have to clean up anything.

04:12.250 --> 04:14.890
But I'm going to go and make prints over here.

04:14.890 --> 04:20.850
So prints and we're going to give the message no null scores found.

04:21.130 --> 04:24.830
And at the last end I'm going to go and put a semicolon.

04:24.830 --> 04:25.710
So that's it.

04:25.710 --> 04:26.710
This is our logic.

04:26.710 --> 04:29.750
We are checking our condition and then we execute.

04:29.750 --> 04:33.830
If the condition is met where we update the table with zero instead of null.

04:33.830 --> 04:36.390
And if the condition is not met then don't do anything.

04:36.390 --> 04:37.630
Just print a message.

04:37.630 --> 04:38.750
Now you might say, you know what?

04:38.790 --> 04:39.670
Why you are doing this.

04:39.710 --> 04:44.070
We just can use this update statement and we don't need the whole if else statements.

04:44.070 --> 04:45.670
So why we are checking in the first place.

04:45.670 --> 04:50.950
I can like each time I run this stored procedure, I go and update all the nulls if they exist to a

04:50.950 --> 04:51.390
zero.

04:51.590 --> 04:55.550
Well, this is not really professional because you are wasting resources.

04:55.550 --> 04:58.270
So each time you run an update statement like this.

04:58.470 --> 05:03.590
So imagine that you have a big table and each time you run your stored procedure, SQL have to go and

05:03.590 --> 05:06.110
check whether there is any nulls and so on.

05:06.110 --> 05:08.350
And this is of course consume resources.

05:08.350 --> 05:12.030
It's way better if you go and check first whether it's really needed.

05:12.030 --> 05:13.750
So that's why we are doing this logic.

05:13.750 --> 05:14.110
Now.

05:14.110 --> 05:17.350
As you can see our stored procedure is getting bigger and bigger.

05:17.750 --> 05:19.110
So we have like two parts.

05:19.150 --> 05:22.630
The first part is preparing and cleaning up the data.

05:22.630 --> 05:25.230
And the second part we are generating reports.

05:25.270 --> 05:28.590
Let's go and update the whole thing and execute it.

05:28.590 --> 05:30.290
And now we have to do it step by step.

05:30.290 --> 05:32.250
So let's check our query over here.

05:32.250 --> 05:35.410
And you can see we have here a null for USA customers.

05:35.410 --> 05:38.850
So let's go first execute it for the USA as a default.

05:38.850 --> 05:40.850
And now let's go and check the messages.

05:40.850 --> 05:43.890
It's saying updating null scores to zero.

05:43.930 --> 05:50.330
That means the first block is executed because SQL did find a customer with a null.

05:50.330 --> 05:54.570
And with that the average of scores can be different than previously.

05:54.570 --> 05:58.490
So we have now more accurate average in our reports.

05:58.490 --> 06:02.810
So if you go and check our query again you can see now we have a zero instead of null.

06:03.090 --> 06:05.930
Let's go and execute it for Germany like this.

06:05.970 --> 06:07.610
And let's go and check the messages.

06:07.610 --> 06:09.890
It says no null scores found.

06:09.890 --> 06:13.970
And that is correct because for Germany we don't have any nulls.

06:14.010 --> 06:18.130
So with that we have created a control flow using the flow statements.

06:18.130 --> 06:21.290
And as you can see, we are not doing any more like simple queries.

06:21.330 --> 06:23.730
We are creating like a mini program.

06:23.730 --> 06:29.410
And now it's like an ETL where first we prepare the data and second, we generate the reports and you

06:29.410 --> 06:33.730
can imagine real projects, how big those stored procedures are going to get, where you have a lot

06:33.770 --> 06:35.810
of tables and a lot of things to do.
